  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x² + 0.6 z²)/2, drawn at 0.4 × loss. The x direction is steeper: steps overshoot in x once learning rate > 0.4 and swings grow (diverge) above 0.8.",
    "Noise added to each slope component has spread 1.5·√((64−b)/(63b)) for batch size b: largest at 1, exactly zero at 64. It is centred on the true slope and comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays it.",
    "Arrows are drawn 2× the actual step (learning rate × slope estimate). Orange: the mini-batch step the ball takes next. Grey: the full-data step, against the true slope. The readout gives the actual step length.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when loss exceeds 8 (diverged). Positions shown on screen are capped near the bowl edge; the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average; single small-batch steps can raise it."
  ]
}